WEBVTT

00:00.400 --> 00:01.844
Welcome to StackHatch.

00:03.250 --> 00:05.848
Right now, your architecture probably
lives in your head.

00:06.374 --> 00:09.450
StackHatch lets you see it, question it,
and shape it.

00:10.500 --> 00:12.086
Say all you have is an idea.

00:12.614 --> 00:16.170
Open a blank canvas and describe it the
way you would to a colleague.

00:19.900 --> 00:22.472
A few seconds later there's a first
draft, and you

00:22.472 --> 00:25.147
can click into any part of it to see why
it's there.

00:29.500 --> 00:31.303
Or maybe the code's already written.

00:31.821 --> 00:35.360
Paste its GitHub link and choose which
folders StackHatch reads.

00:35.800 --> 00:37.990
You see the tokens, the cost and the

00:37.990 --> 00:40.424
GitHub requests before anything is sent.

00:46.550 --> 00:49.407
Each part of the map links to the files
it came from.

00:49.850 --> 00:53.062
Ask about those files, and they go along
with your question.

00:59.350 --> 01:03.231
It works on private repos too, with a
read-only GitHub token.

01:06.550 --> 01:08.363
It works with local folders too.

01:10.950 --> 01:13.895
Leave out what you don't need, then
approve when you're ready.

01:20.550 --> 01:24.184
If you've already written a spec, drop
the Markdown file in, and

01:24.184 --> 01:27.590
your requirements turn into a map you
can review and refine.

01:30.100 --> 01:32.719
Every requirement ends up with a home on
the map.

01:36.500 --> 01:38.626
And when you're not sure about a choice,

01:38.626 --> 01:41.018
click the component and ask for
alternatives.

01:43.240 --> 01:46.616
You see the trade-offs, and if one fits
better, you swap it in.

01:51.900 --> 01:55.719
And if you'd rather do it yourself, you
can add components, connect

01:55.719 --> 01:59.595
them, lock the parts you've settled on,
and let auto-layout tidy up.

02:00.100 --> 02:01.866
Each click tries another layout.

02:07.250 --> 02:11.847
When it's time to share, you can export
an image, SVG, JSON or YAML.

02:12.100 --> 02:15.217
Or have it write a product requirements
document from the map.

02:20.550 --> 02:23.834
It all runs on your own key, from
Anthropic,

02:23.834 --> 02:26.520
OpenAI, Google Gemini or OpenRouter.

02:26.900 --> 02:29.711
There's no account and no StackHatch
server in between.

02:30.152 --> 02:31.743
Your maps stay in your browser.

02:34.600 --> 02:35.526
That's StackHatch.

02:35.865 --> 02:39.553
It's free and open source, so go try it
at stackhatch.io.
